From c6dfbb76f9ba383dc48401f9559fc9bb80dd6e89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AshFall UI <ui@ashfall.local>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 19:21:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Settings: add charging control to the battery page

AshFall #140 v1: the manual charging-separation toggle on
Settings > Battery > Charging control, as the second sequential Settings
patch on the battery page (after #137). One row sits in the PlayTime
category, after the PlayTime row, and opens a sub-screen with the toggle,
the firmware support state and the 80 %/10 % battery-care line.

State lives in persist.ashfall.batt.charge_sep (1 | 0). The switch
applies it immediately, refuses to enable below 20 % or while the charger
is disconnected, and Settings re-applies it from a direct-boot-aware
receiver on LOCKED_BOOT_COMPLETED (BOOT_COMPLETED stays as the fallback),
so separation starts before the first unlock. ACTION_POWER_DISCONNECTED
clears the state and restores charging, and the page registers the same
broadcast while it is open.

The control is the retail vendor factory HAL, called reflectively:
vendor.qti.hardware.factory@1.1::IFactory, falling back to @1.0. The RP5
registers 1.1 only (docs/stock/vintf/manifest.xml), and HIDL lookup is an
exact fqname match, so probing 1.0 alone would report "not supported" on
a working firmware. The bridge probes V1_1 then V1_0 and logs the
interface that answered.

Reflection resolves through Settings' classloader, so the patch also adds
best-effort uses-library entries for vendor.qti.hardware.factory-V1.1-java
and -V1.0-java (required=false, with matching optional_uses_libs so the
build stays green without the vendor module). No captured stock artifact
proves such a shared library exists; if the stub is not linked,
Class.forName fails, the page reports "Not supported on this firmware",
and a native backend is the follow-up. The HAL is not claimed to work
until the device log names the resolved interface.

A missing class or method, a null service and a RemoteException all show
"Not supported on this firmware" and disable the toggle without discarding
the stored choice. Every error path fails open: the property is cleared
and charging restored, a rejected property write is detected by reading it
back, and the boot path does nothing when the stored state is off, so a
missing HAL cannot cause a boot loop.

The row key is ashfall_charging_control: the page already has a Lineage
row keyed charging_control, which would collide in PreferenceGroup.

Copy follows #155: one short sentence per summary, no property names,
sysfs paths or HAL jargon in user text, and the single real warning
(fuel-gauge drift on long separation sessions) kept as two lines.

No QS tile, default off, no dependency on ashfall-hwd.
